{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank You to Morning Brew for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2880,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the Mediterranean shore \nof Egypt some 2000 years ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a scholar was mapping what he thought to \nbe pin pricks in the celestial sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He made careful note of the position of a bright \nstar that hung just above the southern horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But never imagined that he was gazing on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multi-star system the stellar that \nhosted a world much like the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A world that would be humanity’s first destination \nwhen we would finally venture to the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alpha Centauri is the first and brightest\nof the constellation of the centaur, and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42800,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nearest star in our galaxy of 200 billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From Ptolemy of Alexandria's \nfirst record in his Almagest,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50320,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than a millennium and a half passed before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53200,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we started to realize its incredible significance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the end of the 17th century, the star had\nlong since slipped below the horizon of Alexandria"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63520,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in its galactic wanderings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it shone bright from southern India, and\nthere a Jesuit priest peered at Alpha Cen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a newly invented device - the telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Father Jean Richaud saw two stars instead\nof one, these days dubbed Rigel Kentaurus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Tolimar, a binary pair bound in an 80\nyear waltz."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This telescope thing improved over the next\ncentury and a half, allowing astronomers to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "watch alpha-cen sway relative to the background\nstars as Earth orbited the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That parallax revealed the system to be close\n- closer than any other - but still more than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105760,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four light years distant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The two stars were so near and so familiar\n- both within 10% or so of our own Sun’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114400,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115280,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps they also bore planetary systems - and\neven planets like the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dreams of an interstellar humanity started\nwith the twins at the centaur’s foot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our greatest hope of another Earth isn’t\nwith the twins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Forward another century, and a Scotsman named\nRobert Innes photographed the southern stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136640,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Johannesburg to track their galactic\nwanderings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fast-moving alpha-cen system was a prime\ntarget, but Innes found something utterly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unexpected - this wasn’t a binary system\nafter all - it was a trinary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A faint red star crawled on a vast, half-million\nyear orbit around its brighter siblings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the entire history of humanity that orbit\nhas placed this red dwarf closer to the earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165440,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than its companions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it was named Proxima Centauri, or just\nProxima - our solar system’s true nearest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174320,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neighbour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first Proxima’s proximity was mostly\na curiosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Red dwarfs are the smallest, coldest, and\nfaintest of the stellar menagerie."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This one was barely a star at all, really,\nat only 12% of the Sun’s mass and only a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191040,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little larger than Jupiter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192960,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hardly exciting compared to its vibrant, Sun-like\nsiblings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as the decades passed, we began to see\nhow special Proxima really was."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The clue to its uniqueness was in its emission\nlines - sharp spikes in its spectrum resulting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from electron transitions in the atoms and\nmolecules of the star’s atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima’s emissions lines seemed to shift\nback and forth from the wavelengths dictated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218800,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the laws of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it took until … well, now, to finally\nunderstand the mystery of Proxima and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226880,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true magnitude of the importance \nof the Alpha-Cen system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230560,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2016, Spanish astronomer Guillem Anglada-Escudé \nand the Pale Red Dot team figured it out:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239120,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima had a planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or more technically an exoplanet - for extra-solar\nplanet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245440,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets don’t really orbit stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A planet-star pair mutually orbits its shared\ncenter of mass - its barycenter - which is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253680,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually deep inside the star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets make stars wobble, and that motion\ninduces something called Doppler shift in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260880,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star’s emission lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially, the wavelengths of all the star’s\nlight are stretched as the star moves away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from us and compressed as it moves towards\nus, causing emission lines to oscillate in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelength very, very slightly - just like\nwith Proxima."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This way of finding exoplanets is called the\nradial velocity method."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So named because the Doppler effect is only \nproduced by the component of the star’s velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s either directly towards us or away\nfrom us - in the “radial” direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We couldn’t see a planetary system that’s\n“face-on” - fully in the plane of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297920,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the more edge-on the orbits, the more\nmotion there is in the radial direction, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303760,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the more chance of spotting the wiggling\nspectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the most prolific method for finding \nexoplanets has been the transit method,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312560,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are identified by their dimming of their parent\nstar as they cross or transit the face of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318400,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that star from our perspective."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That restricts the transit method to planetary\nsystems that are almost perfectly edge-on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless the Kepler mission found 2600+\nexoplanets this way, and extrapolating from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333520,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that revealed that most stars host planetary\nsystems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that we know this, the radial velocity\nmethod can step in and potentially catch many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343120,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more systems - as long as they’re not perfectly\nface-on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The radial velocity method is emerging as\na competitor to the transit approach due to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rapid improvements in a class of \nhighly specialized spectrograph."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so, equipped with this cool new tool,\nour intrepid team of exoplanet hunters realized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Proxima’s shifting emission lines made\nsense if the star was wobbling in a tight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circle, moving at about a mile per hour over\na period of 11 Days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would then be the length of the year\nof a hypothetical exoplanet responsible for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377280,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a short orbital period, combined with\nthe star’s mass, gave them an orbital radius"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the exoplanet of around 20 times smaller\nthan the Earth’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That sounds perilous, until you realize that\nProxima’s energy output is nearly 600 times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396160,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower than the Sun’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That places the new exoplanet exactly in the\nhabitable zone of the star - just the right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance for the intensity of the star’s\nradiation to potentially allow water to exist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409120,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in liquid form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was one more stunning calculation to\ncome: with the distance between exoplanet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and star combined with the speed of the wobble,\nastronomers could calculate its mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421280,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s practically the same as the Earth’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423680,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe 10-50% more massive, but almost certainly\na rocky world, of the type sometimes inhabited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430880,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by bipedal apes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suddenly, after centuries of pondering the\nalpha-centauri system, it’s full significance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437600,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became clear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nearest habitable exoplanet orbited the\nnearest star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely we’d identified the first port of\ncall for our interstellar future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The discovery of this exoplanet - Proxima\nCentauri B - or Proxima-B - was just the first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455040,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this little star’s surprises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2019 a new team reported the discovery\nof a second exoplanet, its signature also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463280,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "embedded in the slow dance of Proxima’s\nemission lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima C is much bigger than it’s earth-like\nneighbour, at 7 times earth’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also way further out, with 5-year orbit\nat the same distance as Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then in 2020 a third exoplanet was tentatively\nidentified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The prospective Proxima D is just a quarter\nof Earth mass and orbits once every 5 days,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well inside the orbit of Proxima B. That’s\ninterior to the habitable zone, making this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494960,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prospective exoplanet literally boiling hot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Followup observations in January this year\nsomewhat solidified the measurements of Proxima D."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that brings us to today, approaching 2\nmillennia since Ptolemy’s observation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have a bona fide planetary system in at\nleast one of the stars in the Alpha-Cen system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516800,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are admittedly much \nmore tentative detections  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520320,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of planets around its Sun-like siblings - a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neptunish body may have been imaged around\nRigel Kentaurus and an Earth-sized body may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have transited Toliman on an orbit that would\nfry it to a crisp."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536080,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those are quite speculative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Proxima B is almost certainly real, and\nso weirdly similar to the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544800,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it similar enough to have an atmosphere?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548560,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oceans?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549760,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550960,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a closer look."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One very important point is that Proxima B\nis probably tidally locked to its star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s close proximity to the star means strong\ntidal forces, which will have forced the planet’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563280,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation period to be in resonance with its\norbital period."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The simplest case is for the length of the\nplanet’s day to be the same as its year"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571840,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- both 11.2 earth days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would keep the same side of the planet\nfacing the star at all times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One side baked and the other frozen, \nand perpetual twilight at the boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This situation doesn’t sound conducive to\nlife for many reasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, unless there’s a lot of atmospheric\ncirculation, that permanent night could cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere to collapse, freezing to\nthe surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601520,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it may also be that the large temperature "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604800,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "differential drives powerful \natmospheric convection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- planet-wide gales of extraordinary strength\nthat could distribute heat from the day-side"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613520,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the night."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if the planet has significant oceans, these\ncould also distribute heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also possible for a planet to be tidally\nlocked without a perpetual day and night."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other orbital resonances can occur - for example,\n2 days per year, 3 days per 2 years, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632640,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima B may be in one of these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it’s worth noting that any resonance\nbesides permanent day and night would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639840,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive ocean tides, assuming the exoplanet \nhas an ocean to be pulled and squeezed by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647200,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nearby star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, we might actually hope\nfor a 1-to-1 resonance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case, the dark side might be the only\nsurvivable part of the planet, as long as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658720,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s enough atmospheric cycling to keep\nit warm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661920,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order for life to have a chance in this\nsystem, it needs to be protected from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668480,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670080,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t let their size fool you; there’s\nnothing cute about red dwarfs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674320,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can be angry, violent little monsters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677600,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima is no exception."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680160,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s what’s known as a flare star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Massive convection through the star’s body\ngenerates crazy magnetic storms, which can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cause the star to have powerful outbursts\n- flares - that blast high energy particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693520,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and radiation through the planetary system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During flares, Proxima B is blasted with X-rays\nand ultraviolet light and high energy particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A sufficiently thick atmosphere and strong\nplanetary magnetic field could in principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protect any surface dwellers, who would then\nget to enjoy pretty spectacular auroras."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Auroras which may even be visible from Earth\nby a near-future telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite this violence, Proxima really is faint."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At Proxima B’s location there’s enough\nlight to keep water liquid but most of that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730800,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is infrared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s far less visible light, so to our\neyes the star would appear very dim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Photosynthesis in these conditions would be\ndifficult."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Native foliage would probably be black for\nmaximum absorption, and may have to rely on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alternative photosynthetic pathways that can\nmake use of the infrared light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, unlike the Sun which grows brighter\nover time, red dwarfs fade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order for Proxima B’s hypothetical oceans\nand atmosphere to have survived this earlier,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hotter period it would have to have formed\nfurther out and then migrated inwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s not crazy, because planetary formation\nmodels indicate that there wouldn’t have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been enough material so close to the star\nfor it to have formed in that position."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless, we’re focusing in on a relatively\nstringent set of requirements for this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788080,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exoplanet to be able to support life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if it’s a stretch, we need to imagine\nall the ways that life could have formed there,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because that’ll help us build the instruments\nneeded to detect signatures of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re now building and planning a new generation\nof giant telescope - 30 to 100 meters in diameter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which should be sensitive enough to detect\nemission lines from molecules in Proxima B’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere, if it has one, or even the light\nreflected from the planet’s surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both of these may bare the characteristic\nsignatures of life, as we’ve discussed previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately we would want to visit our neighbour\nto pay our respects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828320,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, the mission is already being planned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the breakthrough Starshot program - something\nwe’ve discussed previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The plan is to use a giant laser array to\naccelerate a train of mylar light sails to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841920,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "20% the speed of light in the direction of\nProxima."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’ll each carry a tiny computer chip\nsporting a miniature camera, to take fly-by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850680,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pics as they zip through the system 20 years\nafter launch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no update on when this might launch,\nbut not tomorrow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hopefully one day we’ll be sending people\ninstead of computer ships, to study the system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865680,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and maybe even settle there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Proxima, with its many-trillion-year\nlifespan will far outlive our own Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874560,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s fast forward our story another\nfew millenia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A descendent of humanity stands on the black\ngrassy plains of Proxima B, which sway in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883760,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet-wide gale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Four lights are visible through the thick\natmosphere - the gleaming white twins Tolimar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Rigel Kentaurus, the glowering red orb\nof Proxima herself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s another white star on the horizon, \nbut it’s slowly slipping away on its own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902080,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit around the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps this post-human Proximan will wonder\nif the stories are true - that people began"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there, on a planet around Sol, Alpha-Centauri’s\nnearest neighbor across spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From Wall Street to Silicon Valley, and everywhere\nin between, Morning Brew is a daily email"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "newsletter that curates from major outlets\nso you can get up to speed in just a few minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928000,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Morning Brew covers topics like the environment,\nscience, and tech."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Morning Brew, I actually read about how\nNASA’s James Webb telescope, the largest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and most powerful telescope to ever leave\nEarth, turned in its first photo of outer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940400,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was able to sign up in a few seconds, and\nI can read the daily newsletter in a few minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For more information, visit the link in the\ndescription to sign up for Morning Brew."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our supporters on Patreon give us the financial\nstability we need to keep making this show"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- a stability much like the perpetual daytime\non Proxima B, and the many-trillion year lifespan"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961040,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a red dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today I want to make a special shoutout\nto Daniel Alexiuc, who’s supporting us at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967680,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Daniel, we managed to find a loophole in the\ninternational space treaty and have claimed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ownership of Proxima B. As a sign of our enormous \nappreciation, we’re giving you first pick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979040,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of any continents discovered - assuming it\nhas continents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982160,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Offer is null and void if any sentient natives\nare found."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985280,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Travel expenses are not included."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Otherwise, it’s yours to do with it as you\nwish - name it, claim lordship over it, start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992320,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your galactic empire there, or just use it\nfor weekend getaways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995600,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its just a small token of our gratitude for\nyour support."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998720,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks again, Daniel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comments on the last two\nepisodes: the one on objective collapse theories,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004560,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where wavefunction collapse is explained as\na real, physical process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the one on cosmic strings - topological\ndefects that may span the length of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the objective collapse episode we talked\nabout some field “hitting” the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cause it to collapse, and Kadag asks what\nexactly is doing the hitting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022720,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is we have no idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has to be a field that has a non-linear\ninfluence on the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m afraid it’s hard to give a satisfyingly\nintuitive description of what that means."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033120,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in general it means different branches\nof the wavefunction are able to influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036800,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, which is not the case in standard\nquantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out our episode on the many-worlds or\nEverett-Wheeler telephone for juicy details"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045280,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on non-linear quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yes, one field that might be able to do\nthis is gravity, in which case the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is being “hit” by the non-linearities\nacross the wavefunction introduce by spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057360,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And on the subject of gravity, Jan Wester\nasks why the curvature of spacetime can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063520,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be in a superposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we don’t know for sure that it can’t\n- just that if you try to have a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of curvature you get unresolvable infinities\nat large curvatures that produce hopeless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075840,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contradictions between general relativity\nand quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially, quantum mechanics and quantum\nfield theory assume a well-defined underlying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "framework, upon which all the quantum weirdness\ncan play - those are the dimensions of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088640,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even including relativity and the shifting\nnature of space into time and vice versa,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you still have a consistent if mutable underlying\ngrid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As soon as that stage itself becomes undefined,\neverything becomes much more difficult."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Resolving that has been the major work of\nall of our searches for quantum gravity and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106560,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theories of everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107680,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, on to cosmic strings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109520,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you asked how it is that a topological\ndefect can decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When those cosmic strings radiate gravitational\nwaves, how is the Higgs field supposed to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118160,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smooth itself out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two ways: if the loop shrinks itself down\nto zero size then the Higgs field phase angles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125600,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can match up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, the version of the topological defect\nthat I described in that episode - the simple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vortex of phase angles making a loop around\nthe string - is the most simplistic version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136880,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this defect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138320,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality, the defects are probably more\ncomplicated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather than being a simple knot in the field,\nimagine something more like a shoelace knot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146480,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it can potentially untangle itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a bit of a crappy analogy, so maybe\nI’ll come back to it in another episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brandon Munshaw asks if the Higgs phase angle\nsomething that could theoretically be measured?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or are cosmic strings the only indication\nof a change in phase angle?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165040,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is yes and absolutely no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The phase angle is fundamentally unmeasurable\n- just the the phase of the wavefunction - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a symmetry of the Higgs field and doesn’t\naffect the behavior of the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177200,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the relative phase angle can in principle "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179520,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be measured - that is, we \ncan see the difference in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182800,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase angle between two points in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don’t know how you’d do this besides\nobserving a cosmic string, but I suspect there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189200,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Doctor Scoot asks whether cosmic strings could\nexplain the filaments and voids in the large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale distribution of galaxies in the universe,\nand whether they could explain the cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201120,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microwave background and dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the answer is maybe to the first two - but\nonly if cosmic strings influence the CMB."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These strings may have had sufficient density\nat extremely early times to leave an imprint"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the CMB- on the density fluctuations from\nthe early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221120,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although that hasn’t been detected, at least\nnot yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The later development of large scale structure\nis defined by those density fluctuations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1229040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so in that sense cosmic strings could have\ninfluenced it - but not directly in the sense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234720,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of cosmic strings causing string-like structure\nin galaxy distributions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As for dark matter - extremely unlikely - in\nour most accepted understanding of cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1243600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strings there aren’t anywhere near enough\nof them to account for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But seriously guys one of the weird things we \ntalk about will turn out to be dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1255120,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or Aliens. And that's when our job will be done."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258000,
    "dDurationMs": 17840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let's hope it's never either. But \nwait what if aliens are dark matter!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
